WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:08.223
I've always been interested
in working on systems.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.780
When I'm talking about systems,

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.980
what I mean is that often
we talk about issues

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.075
as sort of siloed things,

00:00:18.075 --> 00:00:19.600
climate's over here,

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.421
poverty's over here,

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:24.700
access to higher education is over here.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.240
But the fact is that
all of those problems,

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.600
they all exist sort of like
within the same system.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.963
And a lot of times
those systems intersect.

00:00:35.963 --> 00:00:38.500
Every time you change a policy,

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.310
you are changing a system.

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:42.420
Rhiana Gunn-Wright wants to tell you

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.912
that the world is more
connected than you might think.

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:48.030
She's the director of climate policy

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.530
at the Roosevelt Institute

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.140
and one of the architects
of the Green New Deal,

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.160
this sweeping comprehensive
climate legislation

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.960
championed by Alexandria Ocasio Cortez,

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.430
and met by political opponents with,

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.730
let's say, some criticism.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.058
After reading the Green New Deal,

00:01:04.058 --> 00:01:06.890
I'm mostly afraid of not being able

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:10.235
to get through this speech
with a straight face.

00:01:10.235 --> 00:01:14.120
The Green New Deal is
expansive, it's comprehensive.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.370
So how does a woman

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.580
with fewer than three years of experience

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.280
in the world of climate justice,

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.590
end up writing one of
the most controversial

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:25.010
and impactful climate
bills in American history?

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:25.900
Because she saw

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.830
that everything in
American power is connected

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:31.683
and she wants everyone
else to see it with her.

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:40.973
Born and raised in Chicago,

00:01:40.973 --> 00:01:43.830
Rhiana Gunn-Wright grew up with asthma.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.080
And I had asthma to the point

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.610
that by the time I think I was in

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.680
like third or fourth grade,

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.855
I was out for like a week every spring.

00:01:53.855 --> 00:01:57.479
I would get sick and I
would be out of school.

00:01:57.479 --> 00:01:59.960
She didn't make the
connection at the time,

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.460
but we now know today that her asthma,

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:03.740
like many others,

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:05.910
was most likely linked
to the air pollution

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:08.640
in her neighborhood where she grew up.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.490
The situation for a lot of other kids

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.970
was that their parents
worked hourly jobs, right?

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:15.110
So every time your child is out of school,

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:17.802
not only are you hurting
their educational outcomes,

00:02:17.802 --> 00:02:20.000
you have mothers trying to figure out

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.017
what to do about childcare.

00:02:22.017 --> 00:02:26.183
These things are all connected.

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:31.420
That connection

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:34.090
has not only shaped
Gunn-Wright's world view,

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.460
but the fundamental tenets
of the Green New Deal.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.090
A lot of what the Green New Deal did

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:43.160
was just connect a lot of conversations

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.640
that were happening already.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.310
Climate justice and
environmental justice activists

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.211
have been talking about how environment

00:02:49.211 --> 00:02:54.020
is larger than polar bears
and ice caps and the Arctic.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:55.910
So it was just essentially

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:59.355
trying to create a policy proposal

00:02:59.355 --> 00:03:02.070
that not just met the
scale of the problem,

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.210
but actually tried to address the problem

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:05.750
as it exists in the real world,

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:10.657
which is a mass of overlapping
and interconnected issues.

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:13.750
The Green New Deal is a 14 page document

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:16.510
and it covers a lot of ground.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:17.630
It not only promises

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:20.657
to get carbon emissions
to net zero by 2050,

00:03:20.657 --> 00:03:22.971
but to add millions of green jobs

00:03:22.971 --> 00:03:24.070
and makes the link

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.475
that climate issues like
fossil fuel emissions

00:03:26.475 --> 00:03:28.430
are justice issues,

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:29.590
since people of color

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:31.900
are more likely to live near power plants

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.630
and other polluting factories,

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.050
and they suffer higher levels of asthma

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:39.133
and greater risk of early
death from air pollution.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.480
While Rhiana's writing
of the Green New Deal

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.140
may be one of the most
influential documents

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.530
in the green space,

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:53.470
she's a relative newcomer when
it comes to climate justice.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:57.967
For her, she sees that as an
asset instead of a hindrance.

00:03:57.967 --> 00:04:00.395
When we talk about climate change,

00:04:00.395 --> 00:04:04.660
most times people are talking
about super scientific stuff,

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:07.080
carbon footprints, parts per million,

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.645
how much carbon is in the atmosphere.

00:04:09.645 --> 00:04:12.950
And there's this feeling I feel
like often in climate policy

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:15.080
that if you don't have a
background in those things,

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:16.459
you can't talk about it.

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:20.560
But the fact is that most
people experience climate change

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.100
as economic effects and health effects.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.561
They experience it as Hurricane Katrina.

00:04:25.561 --> 00:04:29.020
They experience it as the
wildfires in paradise.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:32.070
They experienced it as this heat wave

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:35.219
where they don't have money
to afford air conditioning.

00:04:35.219 --> 00:04:37.950
Climate change is not a partisan thing.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.270
So Rhiana took this pragmatic approach

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:42.400
all throughout her career.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.630
Originally, she was a policy analyst

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.660
for the Detroit Health Department.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:51.053
And Detroit is a hotbed for
environmental injustice.

00:04:51.053 --> 00:04:52.778
When I was working there,

00:04:52.778 --> 00:04:55.742
there was an incinerator
in the middle of the city.

00:04:55.742 --> 00:04:57.598
And around that area,

00:04:57.598 --> 00:05:01.880
asthma rates were like three
times the state average.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:04.760
Residents were complaining
that they couldn't go outside

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.343
'cause it smelled so bad.

00:05:06.343 --> 00:05:10.250
So trying to figure out
what to do about it,

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:14.050
it just became clear that these
things had health effects,

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:16.540
but the reasons that it was happening

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:19.267
were beyond what we could do for people.

00:05:19.267 --> 00:05:22.488
So after working on Abdul Sayed's

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:26.310
2018 Michigan gubernatorial
campaign and losing,

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.340
Rhiana was looking for a job.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:29.173
So she emailed

00:05:29.173 --> 00:05:32.900
Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio
Cortez's campaign manager,

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:34.950
looking for something short term.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:37.130
And when she was offered
a job at New Consensus,

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:38.780
a progressive think tank

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:39.613
They were like,

00:05:39.613 --> 00:05:42.809
"Hey, so we are developing
a Green New Deal,

00:05:42.809 --> 00:05:45.950
which is a mobilization on climate

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:49.464
to restructure the US economy
and reduce emissions."

00:05:49.464 --> 00:05:50.940
In addition,

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:53.018
the idea was to create the lofty goal

00:05:53.018 --> 00:05:58.018
of zero waste, zero poverty
and zero racial wealth gap.

00:05:58.168 --> 00:05:59.707
So they were like,

00:05:59.707 --> 00:06:01.740
"Oh, so you can write this whole thing

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.670
in like four months, right?

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:06.850
And I was like, "No, but maybe

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:09.613
if you give me two research
assistants we can try."

00:06:10.837 --> 00:06:13.143
And so that's what we did.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.390
No one does economic
mobilizations anymore in the US.

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:22.700
Last time we did that was in World War II.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:25.240
So the first thing we had
to figure out was like,

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.370
how do you actually run an
industrial mobilization?

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:29.620
What does that look like?

00:06:30.716 --> 00:06:32.090
That's when Rhiana

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:33.770
brought in other organizations

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:35.150
to not only pitch ideas

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:37.360
for what the Green New
Deal should look like,

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:39.490
but how to shift power structures

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:42.390
and figure out how money
should be dispersed.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.320
So I work with the Green New Deal Network,

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.218
which is essentially a
coalition of movements.

00:06:48.218 --> 00:06:50.379
But what became clear was

00:06:50.379 --> 00:06:52.320
the folks who had been put together

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:55.508
were not climate justice
organizers or advocates.

00:06:55.508 --> 00:06:57.420
That's John Washington.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.700
He's part of the Green New Deal Network.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.580
And he's worked with
Rhiana during his time

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:05.195
at People United for
Sustainable Housing or PUSH.

00:07:05.195 --> 00:07:09.660
Initially, he had doubts about
Rhiana's new deal coalition.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:11.130
They were folks who had had success,

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.580
like Rhiana, in electoral work,

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.410
but who had never done
climate justice work.

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:19.600
And so Rhiana recognized
that there was a gap in that

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:21.340
and really took it all in

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:23.310
and built a relationship with myself

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:26.308
and many others across the
climate justice movement.

00:07:26.308 --> 00:07:28.210
The only way we're gonna figure out

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:31.840
how to do such big intersectional policy

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.040
is to have people from various,

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:36.330
not only viewpoints and expertise,

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.720
but people that are gonna
be affected by this,

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.220
in the room talking to each other,

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:43.493
building relationships
and working together.

00:07:45.225 --> 00:07:47.230
One of those people she listened to

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:48.730
is Lucy Velez.

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:52.254
She lives in Buffalo and she's
a climate justice activist.

00:07:52.254 --> 00:07:54.850
While her story is only
tangentially related

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:56.420
to an environmental issue,

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:58.630
it's actually addressing
the knock-on effects

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:02.800
that spill over into different,
but connected areas of life.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:05.620
I thought, you know, my
cough was just a cough,

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:09.130
all these congestion issues and colds

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:12.711
were just things that normally
most people experience.

00:08:12.711 --> 00:08:14.100
Like Rhiana,

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:17.270
Lucy suffered from persistent
medical issues from pollution,

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:19.630
most likely caused by the Peace Bridge

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:21.370
connecting Buffalo to Canada,

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:24.470
which emitted emissions into
the surrounding neighborhoods.

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:26.120
In the year '98,

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:29.120
I got a house and I
thought that that house

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:31.060
was in good condition.

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:35.550
But in 2016, I was hospitalized

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:38.351
with like a lung and bronchial infection.

00:08:38.351 --> 00:08:40.950
And when Lucy returned from the hospital.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:43.970
I went into a room which
happened to be my office,

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:47.193
and there was this black fuzzy mold.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:51.010
That's when members of PUSH

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:52.580
knocked on Lucy's door one day

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:55.420
and they were able to
help her fix her house.

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:57.645
It took a while to find
different funding sources

00:08:57.645 --> 00:08:59.760
to add up to the amount of money

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.630
that was required to fix Lucy's home

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:03.597
because it was in pretty bad shape.

00:09:03.597 --> 00:09:07.800
The costs to my house was $40,000

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:10.287
to cover the things that I needed.

00:09:10.287 --> 00:09:12.450
So Lucy got her home fixed.

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:15.870
And then a few months later,
she gets a thing from KeyBank

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:17.670
saying that they're redoing her mortgage

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:19.760
because the value of her home has gone up.

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:21.870
Her mortgage payment was doubled.

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:23.090
I didn't have the money.

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:25.912
I couldn't find the money to pay for

00:09:25.912 --> 00:09:29.300
that kind of a jump.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:31.660
And so she used part of her burial fund

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:34.207
to pay her taxes so she
could keep her house.

00:09:34.207 --> 00:09:37.290
That's the problem with Lucy's situation.

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:40.020
It started as a pollution-related issue,

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:43.210
and that issue ended up
affecting her mortgage.

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:44.290
That domino effect

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:47.250
is what the Green New
Deal is trying to address.

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:48.547
That's what Einstein said, right?

00:09:48.547 --> 00:09:50.330
"The consciousness that created a problem,

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:52.270
it's not the consciousness
that's gonna solve it."

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:55.220
So the consciousness of the
nonprofit and private world

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:56.650
is not what's gonna solve the problem.

00:09:56.650 --> 00:09:58.360
It's the consciousness of the community.

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:00.640
And so I think the Green New Deal Network

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:03.720
and the team have started
really to address that.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:05.520
They've started to
build a national network

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:07.260
of organizations that do this work

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:08.260
and that understand

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:10.460
what people in communities
are experiencing.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:14.760
Today's biggest issue

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:16.790
with interconnected consequences?

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:17.950
For four straight days,

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:20.460
the coronavirus claimed
more than 1,000 lives

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:21.370
in this country.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.768
Infections are also still surging.

00:10:23.768 --> 00:10:25.050
But with COVID

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:26.890
spreading throughout the United States,

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:28.280
it seems that climate issues,

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:30.030
including the Green New Deal

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:32.960
are taking a back seat to the pandemic.

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:34.760
But that hasn't stopped opponents

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:36.620
making their feelings known.

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:40.506
What does placing emission
standards on airlines

00:10:40.506 --> 00:10:44.140
have to do with getting
Iowa families and workers

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:47.030
the relief they need right now?

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:47.910
We're here trying to fight

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:50.612
for the man and woman on
the street in our home towns

00:10:50.612 --> 00:10:54.771
and yet they're fighting
for the Green New Deal.

00:10:54.771 --> 00:10:57.810
But Rhiana says that
COVID and climate justice

00:10:57.810 --> 00:10:59.870
have one major thing in common.

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:02.810
They both ravage poor
communities of color.

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:04.810
And it's not a coincidence.

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:08.630
If you look at the
hotspots of COVID deaths,

00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:10.500
they basically mapped on

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:13.550
almost directly onto
frontline communities.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.400
One of the reasons why

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:17.926
COVID ravaged communities of color

00:11:17.926 --> 00:11:20.630
was because we put toxic pollution there

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:24.763
and now COVID is taking
them out at double the rate.

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:30.807
With Joe Biden's

00:11:30.807 --> 00:11:34.220
$1.7 trillion green initiative plan,

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:37.640
some are calling it his own
spin on the Green New Deal.

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:40.303
So it's catching on in the mainstream.

00:11:40.303 --> 00:11:42.540
But Rhiana still knows

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:44.970
she has a long, hard fight ahead of her

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:48.020
because ultimately
changing an entire system

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:51.340
means getting citizens to
realize their own power

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:53.030
over that system.

00:11:53.030 --> 00:11:55.900
We ran into a lot of
people who would talk about

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:58.444
how they felt powerless.

00:11:58.444 --> 00:12:00.110
What do we want?
Climate justice.

00:12:00.110 --> 00:12:02.030
When do we want it?
Now.

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:05.076
But they always have
power in some measure.

00:12:05.076 --> 00:12:09.650
And it's just really important to remember

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:13.343
that you have the right to
gather with other people.

00:12:15.131 --> 00:12:17.180
We all have the ability

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:18.970
to emotionally affect another person,

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.173
to make another person feel connected.

00:12:22.113 --> 00:12:24.123
And that is powerful.

